md`# SoundScript: Sound Dataflow Language

SoundScript is a subset of TypeScript for writing notebook cells, together with a checker that makes a promise about every cell it accepts: given the same inputs, the cell computes the same value, and everything it depends on is visible in the dependency graph. This page teaches the language by example.

Every example the checker accepts is a live cell. Sections 1, 3, 4 and 8 are cells of this module, \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-tutorial\`. Sections 5, 6, 7 and 9 reuse cell names (\`app\` is in all four), so each is a small module of its own (\`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer\`, \`-model\`, \`-workers\`, \`-breakout\`), and this page shows its view and a live table of its cells. Every live cell is compiled to one or two ordinary runtime variables, so it runs in the notebook's own runtime, is edited in the notebook's editor and is saved with the notebook (section "Editing SoundScript cells"). Only the examples the checker must reject, in sections 2 and 8, are source boxes: edit one and it is checked again on every keystroke; a box is not run.

The checker is \`lite\` (\`tools/lang-experiment/lite/\` in lopecode-dev). It is written for this subset without TypeScript's compiler. It, the compiler and the runtime are the module \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript\` (the other tab), which this module imports; the page works from a \`file://\` URL with the network off. This is an experiment, started 2026-11-14; lopecode cells are not written this way today.`
// %%
md`## Why a new language

An Observable cell can do anything JavaScript can. It can read a global the runtime does not know about, call \`Math.random\` or \`Date.now\`, mutate an object another cell returned, or touch the page. The runtime cannot tell whether a cell did any of this, so it does the safe thing: when anything upstream changes it runs everything downstream again, it cannot reuse an old result, and it cannot move a cell to another thread, because the cell might need the page.

A census of the lopecode notebooks (\`plan/substrates-winter-2026.md\`, 2026-11-08) measured how often this happens. Of 8,650 cells, 941 (10.9%) reference a browser or host global that is not one of their inputs (the ES intrinsics, \`Math\` and \`Date\` among them, are not counted, so a cell calling \`Math.random\` is not in the 941); \`document\` appears in 347 cells and \`window\` in 291; 47 cells use \`this\`. Of 780 test cells, a syntactic scan (\`tools/hardened-runtime/lemma-h.mjs\`, 2026-11-09, quoted in the Substrates paper sources) found at most 393 (50.4%) whose support no page access, reflection or hidden state can reach; imports are not followed, so that is an upper bound. For the rest, a passing test is not evidence that it will pass on the next run.

SoundScript starts from the other end: the language only lets you write cells whose behaviour the runtime can know.`
// %%
md`## How it is designed

Four rules, each enforced by the checker, and what each buys:

- **Inputs are parameters.** A cell is an exported function; its parameters are the cells it reads. A body may use its parameters, its own locals, a short prelude and a fixed list of JavaScript intrinsics, and nothing else. So the dependency graph is complete: no global, no \`document\`, no hidden edge.
- **Effects are parameters too.** The clock, randomness, the network, logging and the worker pool are *capabilities*: reserved parameter names (\`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\`, \`log\`, \`invalidation\`, \`compile\`, \`pool\`) that the host supplies. A cell with none of them is deterministic, and a cell with one says so in its signature (section 2).
- **Values on edges are immutable.** A cell may build a value with \`mut\` and change it while it runs, but what it returns is read-only: the checker rejects any write to it, in that cell or any other (section 3). State lives in \`ref\` cells, written only inside \`action(...)\`, and only the host calls an action (sections 4 and 5).
- **The world calls in; cells do not call out.** DOM events reach a cell as typed data through JSX event props, and time arrives through a subscription to a generator (section 9). The view is a value, a JSX element, that a renderer outside the language applies to the page.

It is TypeScript so that editors, syntax and types already exist; the types are also what lets the checker say what crosses to a worker (section 7). It is a strict subset so that every rule above can be checked by reading the source, without running it.`
// %%
md`## What is guaranteed, and what is not

For a cell that \`lite\` accepts: it reads only its parameters, the prelude and the allowed intrinsics; while it runs it writes only values it made, and a ref changes only when the host calls an action; every effect is a named parameter. A cell with no capability parameter, given the same input values, returns the same value. That is what *sound* means on this page.

Not guaranteed:

- \`lite\` is a prototype checked against a reference: 52 of 54 existing fixtures get the same verdict and reason as the TypeScript-based harness, with index reads typed \`T\` as TypeScript does (section "How good is the checker"). Where it approximates a rule, \`lite/README.md\` says so.
- The runtime does not refuse a rejected cell. The rejected cells of sections 3 and 8 were compiled when the notebook was built, and they run; the checker's verdict only keeps them from being memoised. The editor refuses an edit that would leave a cell rejected (section "Editing SoundScript cells").
- A cell may ask for \`now\` or \`random\` and be accepted; it is then not deterministic, and its signature says so. Nothing forces a reducer such as section 9's \`step\` to avoid them.`
// %%
md`## Why this makes notebooks fast

Each rule removes something the runtime would otherwise have to assume. This page measures three consequences (headless Chromium, 2026-11-14):

- **Skip what did not change.** Because values are immutable, a cell whose inputs are the same values as last time can return its last result without running (section 6). In section 9's Breakout, in 1.5 s of play the world changed 89 times in 91 animation frames and the ball view ran 91 times, but the brick view ran once, when a brick broke, and the subscription set ran twice (play, then pause) (\`test_breakout_memo\`).
- **Move work to other threads.** Because an accepted cell touches nothing but its inputs, a group of cells can run in a worker. Section 7 draws a 640×420 Mandelbrot in about 64 ms on the main thread and 15 to 22 ms on a pool of 8 workers (four runs), byte-identical; \`compile\` returns an ordinary typed async function.
- **Patch, do not rebuild.** Views are values, so the renderer compares them with the last ones and changes only what differs. A Breakout frame writes about one \`style\` attribute to the DOM, and a broken brick removes one node.

Not built yet: keeping results across a reload. A deterministic cell's result could be stored in the saved file and reused when its inputs are unchanged; \`plan/substrates-winter-2026.md\`, "Referential transparency → content-addressed caching", describes it.

Sections 1 to 5 teach the language; 6 shows the runtime variables a cell becomes; 7 and 9 are the two performance demonstrations; 8 lists what JSX checking rejects. Edits to live cells, in this module or a section module, are kept when the notebook is saved; edits to the boxes are not.`
// %%
md`## 1. A cell is a function of its inputs

Each \`export\` is a cell. Its parameter names are the cells it depends on, so \`area\` below recomputes when \`width\` or \`height\` changes. The cells below are live cells of this notebook. The table after them is the checker's verdict on this module's SoundScript cells: the **type** column is what \`lite\` inferred, the **value** column is the cell's value in this runtime.`
// %%
//ss: 01-cells.ts
// %%
liveTable({ width, height, area, aspect, scale, half, test_half })
// %%
md`Open \`width\`'s editor (the bar beside the cell), change it to \`() => "640"\` and press Shift-Enter. The editor does not apply it: \`width\` itself checks, but \`area\` would be rejected with \`[inject-type] width: string is not assignable to number\`, and \`aspect\`, which has no annotations and so takes the new type, with \`[type] string used as a number\`. Both are reported on \`width\`'s first line, and \`width\` keeps its old definition. \`half\` would stay ok: \`area\`'s type is still \`number\`, and that is all \`half\` sees. Section "Editing SoundScript cells" gives the rule.`
// %%
md`## 2. What the checker rejects

Five cells below break a rule, one rule each. They are in a box, not live cells: \`width\` and \`area\` are also section 1's names, and a module holds one cell per name. The box is checked, not run; had \`doubled\` run, it would be \`NaN\`, and nothing at run time would say so.`
// %%
viewof src_errors = sourceBox(examples.errors)
// %%
checkBox(src_errors)
// %%
md`\`jittered\` is the accepted form of \`jitter\`. \`random\` is a *capability*: a reserved parameter name (\`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\`, \`log\`, \`invalidation\`, and \`compile\` and \`pool\` from section 7) that the host supplies. A cell that reads the clock or a random source says so in its parameter list, so the dependency graph shows every cell that can differ between two runs.`
// %%
md`## 3. Local mutation with \`Mut\`

Writes are allowed on a value made by \`mut(...)\`, inside the cell that made it. \`freeze\` ends the writes and gives back a plain value. The checker tracks the \`Mut\` binding, not the object, so a \`Mut\` that is returned, aliased, used after \`freeze\`, or captured by a function that escapes is an error. A local helper that is only called may capture one; RULES.md forbids that too, and \`lite\` allows it on purpose.`
// %%
//ss: 03-mut.ts
// %%
liveTable({ samples, histogram, leak, late })
// %%
md`\`leak\` and \`late\` are rejected and still run, because nothing at run time consults the checker. The editor will not apply an edit to either until the edit makes it pass.`
// %%
md`## 4. State, actions and JSX

There is no \`viewof\` and no \`mutable\`. State is a cell returning \`ref(initial)\`. A parameter typed \`Ref<T>\` gets the reference, which has \`set\` and \`update\` and no read; a parameter without \`Ref\` gets the current value and recomputes when it changes. Writes are legal only inside \`action(...)\`, and only the host (here, the renderer) calls an action.

The view is a cell too. A \`.tsx\` source may use JSX; each element is checked against the declared props of its tag (see the appendix), and an event prop must be an \`Action\` with the right argument types.`
// %%
//ss: 04-counter.tsx
// %%
view = ss.mount(app, this)
// %%
liveTable({ count, increment, clear, app })
// %%
md`\`view\` is an ordinary Observable cell, not SoundScript: \`ss.mount(app, this)\` hands the element value to the renderer. Each JSX node in \`app\` became an \`h(tag, props, ...children)\` call (\`lite/jsx/lower.ts\`). \`h\` returns a frozen \`{tag, props, children}\` value. The renderer (\`lite/jsx/render.ts\`, 75 lines) is outside the checked language: it creates the DOM once, then patches it in place each time \`app\` produces a new value (skipping any element that is the same object as last time, and matching children by \`key\` when they all have one), and it calls the actions when DOM events arrive.

\`count\` is a ref cell, so it is two variables: \`viewof count\` holds the handle that \`Ref<number>\` parameters receive, and \`count\` holds the value. The handle takes the \`viewof\` name so the notebook's tools group the pair as one cell, the way they group \`viewof x\` and \`x\`: the page shows \`count\`, and the editor opens the one source.`
// %%
md`## 5. Every write as a reducer

When state has several fields, two actions that each \`set\` part of it can interleave. Writing every change as \`state.update(s => step(s, event))\` makes one function, \`step\`, responsible for all transitions. \`step\` is an ordinary function cell, so \`test_undo\` tests it with no DOM.`
// %%
reducer_view = ss.mount(reducer_app, this)
// %%
moduleTable(reducer_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer")}).`
// %%
md`## 6. The variables a cell becomes

A cell is source. The compiler (\`lite/toolchain/ss-toolchain.ts\`) turns each one into one or two *variables* of the notebook's Observable runtime, the objects that hold a value and recompute when an input changes. The table under the app lists every SoundScript variable of \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model\` with its inputs and how many times its body has run. Press **bump** and watch which ones run again.`
// %%
model_view = ss.mount(model_app, this)
// %%
variableTable(model_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model")}).`
// %%
md`Five kinds of variable, read off that table:

- **A plain cell** is one variable with the cell's name. Its inputs are \`ss\` (the standard library) and its parameter names. It is *memoised*: when every input is the same value (\`Object.is\`) as on its last run, the body does not run and the variable keeps its last value. Observable still recomputes the readers, which then hit their own memo. The **runs** column counts body runs. This is sound because the checker only accepts cells that are functions of their inputs and never mutate a value they did not make; in ordinary Observable a cell can mutate an object in place and keep its identity, and memoising would hide the change. Three kinds are not memoised. A cell the checker rejects, such as the mistakes shown live in sections 3 and 8, is not covered by that argument, so it runs every time. A cell that takes \`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\` or \`log\` can differ between two runs on the same inputs (section 2). A cell that reads a generator's values may be lent the same buffer mutated between yields (\`yield borrow(buf)\`, \`RULES.md\`).
- **A ref cell** (\`count\`) is two. \`viewof count\` holds the handle that \`Ref<number>\` parameters get, made once. \`count\` holds the current value and yields again after every committed write, so \`doubled\` recomputes on **bump** and \`bump\` does not.
- **A capability** is a variable of the core module: \`now\` is \`ss_now\`, \`random\` is \`ss_random\`, and so on. \`stamped\` takes \`now\`, so its inputs list \`ss_now\`.
- **A cell that calls \`compile\`** has the cells it names in \`refs\` as extra inputs, after its parameters. \`areaFn\` takes \`scale\` this way, although \`scale\` is not one of its parameters (section 7). Its \`compile\` parameter is \`ss_compile\`, which a module that compiles defines for itself, because \`compile\` reads that module's cells.
- **A generator taken as a \`Source\`** (section 9) is one variable holding a handle. The runtime never steps it; only subscriptions do.

Whether a cell is memoised, and whether a generator is a handle, depends on the other cells of its module, so an applied edit compiles again any other cell whose form it changes. None of this is typed in the language: a cell cannot see a variable. The typed form was sketched on 2026-11-14 and is not built (\`lite/NOTES-reflection.md\`):

\`\`\`ts
interface Variable<T, Args extends readonly unknown[]> {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly definition: (...args: Args) => T;
  readonly inputs: { readonly [K in keyof Args]: Variable<Args[K], readonly unknown[]> };
  readonly value: RefCell<T>;                 // reading it is a graph edge
}
interface Module<C extends Record<string, unknown>> {   // C: cell name -> value type
  variable<K extends keyof C & string>(name: K): Variable<C[K], readonly unknown[]>;
  redefine<K extends keyof C & string>(name: K, cell: Cell<C, C[K]>): Action;  // keeps the type
  define<K extends string, T>(name: K, cell: Cell<C, T>): Action;              // recheck downstream
}
\`\`\`

\`C\` would be generated from the types \`lite\` already infers, the **type** column. \`lite\` cannot check this sketch yet: it lacks bounds (\`K extends keyof C\`), \`keyof\` and indexed access, rest parameters typed by a tuple, and inferring \`Args\` from a parameter list. \`compile\`, next, is the one place a cell names other cells today, and \`lite\` types it by special case.`
// %%
md`## 7. compile: cells as a function that runs on workers

\`compile\` takes three lists of cell names and returns an ordinary async function:

\`\`\`ts
export const inPool = (compile: Compile, pool: Pool) =>
  compile({
    inputs: ["y0", "y1"],                  // the function's argument: { y0, y1 }
    refs: ["width", "height", "maxIter"],  // read from the graph each time this cell runs
    outputs: ["rows"],                     // the result: Promise<{ rows }>
    options: { runtime: pool },            // a worker pool; leave it out to run on the main thread
  });
\`\`\`

The *island* is every cell upstream of the outputs, stopping at the inputs and refs; here it is \`rows\`. Island cells are ordinary cells: on the main thread \`rows\` computes one row, for the defaults \`y0 = 0\` and \`y1 = 1\`.

**The type** is computed from the cell graph. \`inPool\` is \`(args: { y0: number; y1: number }) => Promise<{ rows: Uint8ClampedArray }>\` (the type column below), and \`image\` uses it without an annotation. With \`C\` the module's map from cell name to value type, as in section 6, it is \`(args: Pick<C, Inputs>) => Promise<Pick<C, Outputs>>\`. TypeScript cannot check that without \`C\`, so \`lite\` types the call itself, and the oracle cannot cross-check it. The names must be literal strings; anything else is \`unsupported\`.

**The reactive link.** Each name in \`refs\` is a hidden input of the cell that calls \`compile\` (section 6). Move the iterations slider: \`maxIter\` changes, \`inPool\` runs again and returns a new function with the new value inside it, and \`image\` recomputes because its input changed. Work still queued for the old function is dropped.

**The rules**, each a rejection in \`fixtures/lite-compile/reject-compile\`: a name must be a cell; an island cell must be accepted, synchronous, and take no capability and no ref cell (put the ref cell in \`refs\`); inputs, refs and outputs must survive \`structuredClone\` (no functions, refs or actions); \`options.runtime\` must be a \`Pool\`; and a call must match the computed argument type. \`compile\` refuses to run unless the checker accepts the calling cell and every island cell (\`test_compile_refuses_unchecked\`). That refusal is the only guard: the island's erased code runs in the worker unsandboxed, and keeping it to its inputs is the checker's job.`
// %%
workers_view = ss.mount(workers_app, this)
// %%
workers_picture = {
  const canvas = this ?? htl.html`<canvas class="lite-mandel">`;
  canvas.width = workers_width;
  canvas.height = workers_height;
  const data = new ImageData(workers_width, workers_height);
  data.data.set(workers_image);
  canvas.getContext("2d").putImageData(data, 0, 0);
  return canvas;
}
// %%
workers_redraw = {
  // workers_maxIterAt runs in the same pass as inPool, so this is maxIter to picture
  workers_picture;
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-readout">${workers_width}×${workers_height}, maxIter ${workers_maxIter}: ${(performance.now() - workers_maxIterAt).toFixed(0)} ms from maxIter to the picture</div>`;
}
// %%
workers_maxIterAt = (workers_maxIter, performance.now())
// %%
moduleTable(workers_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers")}).`
// %%
md`Try, in the workers module's editor: in \`image\`, change \`inPool(b)\` to \`inPool({ y0: b.y0 })\`, and the edit is refused because the argument has no \`y1\`. Or add \`now\` as a parameter of \`rows\`, and it is refused because \`inPool\` would be rejected: an island cell takes a capability.

The same island and the same bands, timed on the main thread (\`onMain\`) and in the pool (\`inPool\`) in this browser, after three calls of each to warm up. The numbers are taken while the page loads, so press **measure again** for a quieter run:`
// %%
viewof remeasure = Inputs.button("measure again")
// %%
benchmarkView = htl.html`<table class="lite-panel">
<tr><td>workers in the pool</td><td>${benchmark.poolSize} (navigator.hardwareConcurrency ${benchmark.hardware})</td></tr>
<tr><td>bands (calls) per image</td><td>${benchmark.bands}</td></tr>
<tr><td>main thread, median of ${benchmark.runs}</td><td>${benchmark.mainMs.toFixed(0)} ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>worker pool, median of ${benchmark.runs}</td><td>${benchmark.poolMs.toFixed(0)} ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>same bytes from both</td><td>${benchmark.same}</td></tr>
</table>`
// %%
md`## 8. What JSX checking rejects

\`fixtures/lite-jsx/reject-jsx\`: 11 cells, each with one JSX mistake, and the supporting cells they use. Eleven of them are live cells of this module, and they run, because nothing at run time consults the checker.`
// %%
//ss: 08-reject-jsx.tsx -bad_free_component
// %%
liveTable({ n, inc, setN, Badge, NotElem, bad_attr_type, bad_unknown_attr, bad_unknown_element, bad_handler_not_action, bad_handler_wrong_args, bad_component_props, bad_component_missing_prop, bad_component_not_elem, bad_mut_in_jsx, bad_child_object, ok_all })
// %%
md`The twelfth mistake, \`bad_free_component\`, uses a component that does not exist. As a live cell it would name a variable the module does not define, so it is shown in a box, checked on its own:`
// %%
viewof src_free_component = sourceBox(examples.rejectJsx.split("\n").find((l) => l.includes("bad_free_component")))
// %%
checkBox(src_free_component, { tsx: true })
// %%
md`## 9. A game loop: Breakout

Section 5's split, applied to a game. \`step(world, event)\` is the whole game: a pure function of the world and one event (\`tick\` with the milliseconds since the last frame, \`aim\` with the pointer's x, or \`reset\`). The world is a ref cell, and three actions, \`tick\`, \`aim\` and \`reset\`, are each one call of \`step\`.

What calls \`tick\` is a **subscription**. The clock, \`ticks\`, is an ordinary generator cell: it yields the time since its last step and writes nothing. A parameter typed \`Source<number>\` gets a handle to that generator instead of its values, the way \`Ref<T>\` gets a handle to state instead of its value. \`on(ticks, tick)\` is a value describing "deliver each value of \`ticks\` to \`tick\`", and \`subscriptions\` returns the set of them the game wants now: one while playing and not over, none otherwise. The standard library (\`lite/stdlib/runtime.ts\`, \`hold\` and \`release\`) keeps a subscription running while the same source and action stay in the set, starts a fresh generator for one that appears, and stops one that leaves. It steps a sync generator once per animation frame. A set is released when the cell that returned it runs again, and, because the runtime does not resolve \`invalidation\` for a deleted variable, when that cell is deleted (\`ss_lifecycle\` in the core). So pause and game over both stop the clock. \`subscriptions\` reads \`over\`, which reads \`world\`. While the game runs, \`over\` stays \`false\`, so memoisation (section 6) keeps \`subscriptions\` from running again, and if it did run, nothing would restart. Subscriptions are counted across cells, so a cell that passes the set on does not start a second clock.

The \`ticks\` row below shows type \`number\` and a handle as its value: the type is what the generator yields, and the value is the handle, because the runtime never steps a generator that is taken as a \`Source\`. A parameter that names \`ticks\` without \`Source\` would get that handle as a number, so the checker rejects it (\`[inject-source]\`).

A generator cannot be the loop by itself: its body would have to write the world, and writes are legal only inside \`action(...)\`. \`fixtures/lite-game/reject-game\` holds that generator (\`[ref-write]\`), a cell calling \`tick(16)\` itself (\`[action-call]\`), \`on\` given a plain function or an action of the wrong type (\`[type]\`), and a \`Source\` that names a cell which is not a generator, or yields another type (\`[inject-source]\`).

**Components are cells.** \`bricks\`, \`ball\` and \`paddle\` each select one part of the world, and \`bricksView\`, \`paddleView\` and \`ballView\` draw one part each; \`app\` puts the three elements in a frame. Because cells are memoised on their inputs (section 6), \`step\` returning the same \`bricks\` array when nothing was hit means \`bricksView\`'s body does not run, and \`app\` gets the same element object as before. The renderer (\`lite/jsx/render.ts\`) patches the DOM in place and skips a subtree whose element is the same object as last time, so the 24 bricks cost one comparison per frame. Children that all carry \`key\` are matched by key, so a broken brick removes one node. \`test_breakout_memo\` measures it. In this runtime the world changes once per animation frame while playing (121 changes in 121 frames over 2 s, headless Chromium, 2026-11-14).

The board is \`div\`s positioned by \`style\` strings, since the checked JSX tags (appendix) have no SVG or canvas drawing. A transparent \`canvas\` on top supplies \`onPointerMove\`.`
// %%
breakout_view = ss.mount(breakout_app, this)
// %%
moduleTable(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-breakout](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-breakout")}).`
// %%
md`Press **play** and move the pointer over the board. \`test_replay\` plays 600 frames with the paddle following the ball, twice, without a browser or a clock, and checks that the two runs end with the same score and ball position and no life lost. That is the property \`step\` being pure buys: a recorded event list replays to the same game.

Try, in the breakout module's editor: give \`step\` a \`now: Clock\` parameter and use it for \`dt\`. The checker accepts it, because \`now\` is a capability any cell may ask for, but \`step\` now has a capability in its signature and \`test_replay\` no longer has to agree with itself. Nothing in \`lite\` requires \`step\` to be pure beyond what its parameters show; section "Not built" describes labelling that.`
// %%
md`## Editing SoundScript cells

A live cell is compiled one cell at a time (\`lite/toolchain/ss-toolchain.ts\`). The result is one runtime variable, or two for a ref cell, whose definition is ordinary JavaScript. It also depends on the rest of the module (section 6), so an applied edit compiles again any other cell whose form it changes. Its first comment is the marker \`/*{lang:"ss"}*/\`, the types are kept as \`/*ss:…*/\` comments, JSX as \`/*ss<…*/\` beside its lowered \`h(...)\` calls, and the text around the function (the comments above it, \`export const name = \`) in a \`/*ss^{…}*/\` header. So the typed source is recovered from the definition alone, and a definition survives anything that keeps a function's text, such as saving the notebook. \`test_ss_roundtrip\` checks every live variable: decompile, compile again, and compare the text.

This notebook carries its own copy of \`@tomlarkworthy/editor-5\` with one addition, a \`cell_languages\` plugin point (its cell \`languageOf\`). The core's cell \`ss_registration\` registers SoundScript there. For a cell whose variables carry the marker, the editor then:

- shows the typed source, with TypeScript and JSX highlighting;
- reports the checker's diagnostics as you type;
- on Shift-Enter or the apply button, checks the whole module with the edited text in place of the cell, and applies the edit only if the cell is \`ok\` and no cell that was \`ok\` before becomes rejected, unsupported or blocked. A diagnostic that belongs to another cell is shown on the edited cell's first line, naming that cell.

A refused edit changes nothing: the cell keeps its old definition and value, and the editor keeps the typed text with its diagnostics, so it can be fixed or abandoned. A new cell typed in this module whose source starts with \`export\` is SoundScript too.`
// %%
md`## Why four sections are modules

A module is one namespace, and the example sources (\`lite/notebook/examples/\`) are shared with the fixtures and the CLI tests (\`lite/notebook/verify.ts\` checks they are equal), so they keep their names. Two ways to make every section live were possible: rename the cells of each section (\`reducer_step\`, \`game_step\`), which changes the text the reader edits and the fixtures check, or give each colliding section its own module. This notebook does the second. The cost: a section's cells are edited in its own module, opened by the link under its table, and this page imports each section's \`app\` under another name (\`reducer_app\`, \`breakout_app\`).

Section 2's errors stay in a box because \`width\` and \`area\` are section 1's names; section 8's \`bad_free_component\` stays in a box because, live, it would name a variable that does not exist.`
// %%
md`## Language reference

One entry per construct, as \`lite\` checks it on 2026-11-14. The rules come from \`tools/lang-experiment/RULES.md\` (v0); entries marked *lite* are additions made in \`lite\` this session and are not in RULES.md. The bracketed name is the rule a violation reports, as in the cell tables above.`
// %%
md`### Cells

A source file holds cells and type declarations, nothing else (\`[module-level]\`).

| form | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`export const x = (a, b) => …\` | a cell \`x\` with inputs \`a\` and \`b\`; \`export let\`, a \`function\` expression and \`export function x(a, b) {…}\` are the same |
| \`export const x = async (a) => …\` | value type \`T\` for a body returning \`Promise<T>\` |
| \`export const x = function* (a) {…}\` | a generator cell; value type \`Y\` for \`Generator<Y>\`; generators cannot be arrows |
| \`export const x = (): RefCell<T> => ref(v)\` | a ref cell: state, value type \`T\` (see Refs) |
| \`export const f = () => (n: number) => n * 2\` | a cell whose value is a function: two arrows, the outer one lists the inputs |
| \`type T = …\`, \`interface I {…}\`, \`import type\` | allowed at top level, not cells |

An export that is not a function is \`[cell-not-function]\`; two cells with one name are a parse error (the table is replaced by the message). Two cells that read each other are \`[cycle]\`. A cell named \`test_*\` is a test and passes if it does not throw.`
// %%
md`### Inputs

Each parameter of a cell names what it reads. What it receives depends on the name and the annotation:

| parameter | receives | a dependency on the value? |
|---|---|---|
| \`a\` or \`a: T\`, naming a cell | the cell's current value; an annotation must accept it (\`[inject-type]\`) | yes |
| \`count: Ref<T>\`, naming a ref cell | the handle, with \`set\` and \`update\` and no read (\`[inject-ref]\` if not a ref cell of \`T\`) | no |
| \`ticks: Source<T>\`, naming a sync generator cell (*lite*) | a handle for \`on(...)\` (\`[inject-source]\` if not a generator yielding \`T\`) | no |
| a capability name (below) | the host's object for that effect | no |

Once any cell takes a generator as a \`Source\`, a parameter reading it without \`Source\` is \`[inject-source]\`, because it would receive the handle. Any other name is \`[inject-unknown]\`; a destructuring pattern is \`[inject-pattern]\`. A body may not use a cell's name without taking it as a parameter (\`[cell-by-name]\`).

Types: a parameter needs a type from an annotation or from its context (\`[untyped-parameter]\`); a type name that is not declared is \`[unknown-type]\`. Most other mismatches, such as a \`string\` where a \`number\` is wanted, are \`[type]\`; a call with the wrong number of arguments is \`[arity]\`.`
// %%
md`### Capabilities

Reserved parameter names; the host supplies one per cell that asks.

| name | type | effect |
|---|---|---|
| \`now\` | \`Clock = () => number\` | the time in ms |
| \`random\` | \`Random = () => number\` | a random number |
| \`fetch\` | \`Fetch\` | network: \`(url, init?) => Promise<FetchResponse>\` |
| \`log\` | \`Log = (...args) => void\` | a line in the program's log |
| \`invalidation\` | \`Promise<void>\` | resolves when this run of the cell is replaced |
| \`compile\` | \`Compile\` (*lite*) | turns a group of cells into a function (section 7) |
| \`pool\` | \`Pool\` (*lite*), \`{ size: number }\` | the page's worker pool, for \`compile\`'s \`runtime\` |

A cell that takes none of these is deterministic.`
// %%
md`### Names a body may use

Its parameters, its own locals, the prelude (\`ref\`, \`action\`, \`mut\`, \`freeze\`, \`borrow\`, \`snapshot\`, \`equals\`, and \`on\` in *lite*), and these intrinsics: \`Object Array Math JSON Number String Boolean BigInt Symbol Map Set Error TypeError RangeError RegExp Promise\`, the typed arrays, \`ArrayBuffer DataView TextEncoder TextDecoder Infinity NaN undefined isNaN isFinite parseInt parseFloat encodeURIComponent decodeURIComponent encodeURI decodeURI atob btoa structuredClone URL URLSearchParams\`. RULES.md allows all of these, but \`lite\` has no declarations yet for \`Symbol\`, \`DataView\`, \`TextEncoder\` and \`TextDecoder\`: using one is *unsupported*, and \`new\` on one is \`[new-non-intrinsic]\`; \`new Promise\` is also unsupported. Any other name is \`[free-name]\`. Some members of allowed objects are banned, \`Math.random\` among them (\`[banned-member]\`); a member a declared type does not have is \`[no-member]\`, and any member of an intrinsic that \`lite\`'s declarations leave out is reported *unsupported*, not ok.

Not available at all: \`Date\`, \`console\`, \`window\`, \`document\`, \`globalThis\`, \`fetch\` (use the capability), timers, \`eval\`, \`Function\`.`
// %%
md`### Immutable values

Values are read-only. Rejected:

| construct | rule |
|---|---|
| \`a.b = …\`, \`a[i] = …\`, \`++\`/\`--\`/\`+=\` on a member | \`[member-write]\` |
| \`push pop shift unshift splice sort reverse fill copyWithin set add delete clear\` on an array, map, set or typed array | \`[mutating-method]\` |
| \`delete a.b\` (allowed on a \`Mut\`) | \`[no-delete]\` |
| \`Object.assign(existing, …)\`; \`Object.assign({}, …)\` is reported unsupported, use a spread | \`[object-assign]\` |
| \`===\`, \`!==\`, \`==\`, \`!=\` on two non-primitive values; use \`equals(a, b)\` | \`[strict-eq-object]\` |
| reassigning a \`const\` | \`[const-assign]\` |
| \`var\`, \`class\`, \`this\`, \`arguments\` | \`[no-var]\`, \`[no-class]\`, \`[no-this]\`, \`[no-arguments]\` |
| \`new\` on anything but an allowed intrinsic | \`[new-non-intrinsic]\` |
| \`any\`, \`as\` (except \`as const\`), \`x!\`, \`@ts-ignore\`, \`enum\`, \`namespace\`, \`import()\` | \`[no-any]\` (inside a cell; not checked in a top-level \`type\`), \`[no-cast]\`, \`[no-non-null]\`, \`[no-ts-ignore]\`, \`[no-enum]\`, \`[no-namespace]\`, \`[no-dynamic-import]\` |

Allowed: local \`let\` and reassigning it, loops, \`throw\`, \`try\`/\`catch\`. A \`let\` may be assigned from a nested function only if that function is a local helper called directly and never passed on (\`[let-capture]\`). Reading a property or index of a value that may be \`undefined\` is \`[possibly-undefined]\`. On this page an index read \`a[i]\` has type \`T | undefined\`, so \`xs[0] * 2\` is rejected; narrow it or write \`xs[0] ?? 0\`. (The oracle comparison in "How good is the checker" uses \`T\`, as TypeScript does.)

Not modelled, reported *unsupported*: \`switch\`, \`for…in\`, \`instanceof\`, \`??=\` \`&&=\` \`||=\`, destructuring assignment (\`[a, b] = [b, a]\`), tagged templates, the comma operator, \`satisfies\`, a \`type\` declared inside a body, \`yield*\`, \`for await\`, and, in a function holding a \`Mut\`, \`try/finally\` and labelled \`break\`.`
// %%
md`### Local mutation: \`mut\`, \`freeze\`

\`const out = mut(new Float32Array(n))\` gives a \`Mut<Float32Array>\` that the cell may write; \`freeze(out)\` ends the writing and gives the plain value (section 3).

| rule | |
|---|---|
| \`mut(e)\` only as the initializer of a local \`const\` | \`[mut-bind]\` |
| \`e\` must be fresh: an array or object literal, a spread copy, \`new\` on an intrinsic, or an intrinsic result marked fresh | \`[mut-not-fresh]\` |
| a \`Mut\` may not be returned, yielded, aliased, put in a literal, or captured by an escaping function | \`[mut-escape]\`, \`[mut-capture]\` |
| no use after \`freeze\` | \`[mut-after-freeze]\` |
| writes only at the first level (\`out[i] = …\`, not \`out.a.b = …\`) | \`[mut-deep-write]\` |
| a view (\`subarray\`) of a \`Mut\` buffer may only be passed straight to a non-retaining intrinsic, not bound to a name | \`[mut-view]\` |
| only a \`Mut\` made by this function may be frozen, not a \`Mut\` parameter | \`[mut-freeze-param]\` |

A helper may take a \`Mut<T>\` parameter and write it; \`lite\` also lets a non-escaping local helper capture one, which RULES.md does not (\`reject-mut-capture\` in "How good is the checker").`
// %%
md`### Lending a buffer: \`borrow\`, \`snapshot\`

A generator cell may keep a \`Mut\` across yields and \`yield borrow(buf)\`; readers get a \`Borrowed<T>\`, read-only, valid during their own synchronous run. \`borrow\` appears only as the operand of \`yield\` (\`[borrow-placement]\`). A reader may not keep a \`Borrowed\` across \`await\` (\`[borrowed-await]\`), return it, store it or capture it in a nested function such as a \`map\` callback (\`[borrowed-escape]\`); \`snapshot(b)\` copies it to a plain \`T\`. A cell that reads a generator is not memoised, because the lent buffer changes in place between yields.`
// %%
md`### State: \`ref\`, \`action\`

| construct | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`export const count = (): RefCell<number> => ref(0)\` | a ref cell; \`ref(...)\` only as the returned value of a sync, non-generator cell (\`[ref-placement]\`) |
| \`count\` or \`count: number\` as a parameter | the current value; the reader runs again after each committed write |
| \`count: Ref<number>\` as a parameter | the handle: \`set(v)\`, \`update(f)\`, no read |
| \`action((by: number) => count.update((c) => c + by))\` | an \`Action<[number]>\`; writes are legal only lexically inside \`action(...)\` (\`[ref-write]\`) |

A cell body never calls an action (\`[action-call]\`); an action may call another action inside its own body. A cell returns an action, puts it in a record, passes it to an \`Action\` parameter, a JSX event prop or \`on(...)\`; an action where something else is expected is \`[action-pass]\`. The host calls it. Writes made by one action call are committed together after it returns.`
// %%
md`### Subscriptions: \`Source\`, \`on\` (*lite*)

| construct | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`export const ticks = function* (now: Clock) {…}\` | a sync generator cell used as an event source; writes nothing; an async generator cannot be a \`Source\` yet |
| \`ticks: Source<number>\` | a handle to the generator, not its values |
| \`on(ticks, tick)\` | a \`Sub\`: deliver each value of \`ticks\` to \`tick\`, which must be an \`Action<[number]>\` (\`[type]\`) |
| a cell returning \`Sub[]\` | the subscriptions the program wants now |

The standard library keeps one running generator per (source, action) held by any cell, starts one when it first appears in a set, and stops it (\`return()\`) when no set holds it. A sync generator is stepped once per animation frame. Section 9.`
// %%
md`### Workers: \`compile\` (*lite*)

\`compile({ inputs: [...], refs: [...], outputs: [...], options: { runtime: pool } })\`, every list a literal array of cell names. The *island* is the cells needed to compute \`outputs\` from \`inputs\`. The value is a function of type \`(args: Pick<C, inputs>) => Promise<Pick<C, outputs>>\`, where \`C\` maps cell names to value types; it runs on the pool when \`runtime\` is given, on the page otherwise.

| rule | |
|---|---|
| island cells accepted, synchronous, no capability, no ref cell (name it in \`refs\`) | \`[compile-island]\` |
| inputs, refs and outputs survive \`structuredClone\`: no functions, refs, actions or borrowed values | \`[compile-boundary]\` |
| \`options.runtime\` is a \`Pool\` | \`[compile-options]\` |

Each name in \`refs\` is a hidden input of the calling cell, so a change gives a new function. \`compile\` refuses to run it unless the checker accepts the calling cell and every island cell. Section 7.`
// %%
md`### Views: JSX (*lite*)

In a \`.tsx\` source, \`<tag prop={…}>children</tag>\` is a value of type \`Elem\`; spread attributes (\`{...props}\`) are unsupported. A fragment \`<>…</>\` groups children. A capitalised local function returning \`Elem\`, \`const Card = (p: { title: string }) => <div>{p.title}</div>\`, is a component used as \`<Card title="x" />\`; one that returns something else is \`[type]\`. Allowed tags: \`div span p label strong fieldset legend output button input canvas\`. Props: \`class style title id key\` on all; \`onClick disabled\` on \`button\`; \`type name value min max step checked disabled onInput onClick\` on \`input\`; \`width height onPointerDown onPointerMove onPointerUp onPointerCancel\` on \`canvas\`. A prop not declared for the tag is \`[excess-property]\` (so are \`data-*\` and \`aria-*\`); a tag not in the list, such as \`a ul li img select textarea section\`, is \`[no-member]\`. Event props take an \`Action\`: \`onClick\` an \`Action<[]>\`, \`onInput\` an \`Action<[string]>\`, pointer events an \`Action<[PointerData]>\` with \`x y t id buttons\`. Children are elements, strings, numbers, booleans, \`null\`/\`undefined\`, or arrays of elements. The full declarations are in the appendix; section 8 shows each rejection.`
// %%
md`### How live cells are evaluated (this page)

Not part of the checked language; what the standard library (\`lite/stdlib/runtime.ts\`) does when a compiled cell runs.

- A cell runs when an input it reads changes. A plain cell whose inputs are all the same values (\`Object.is\`) as last time does not run and keeps its value (memoised); excluded are cells the checker rejects, cells reading a generator and cells taking \`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\` or \`log\`.
- A generator cell is stepped by the Observable runtime, at most once per animation frame, unless it is taken as a \`Source\`, in which case only subscriptions step it.
- Rejected cells that were compiled into the notebook still run; the editor refuses edits that would leave a cell rejected, and \`compile\` refuses to run an island the checker did not accept.
- The view returned by a mounted cell is applied to the DOM by patching: same tag at the same position reuses the node, an element that is the same object as last time is skipped, children that all carry \`key\` are matched by key.`
// %%
md`## How good is the checker

Measured 2026-11-14 with \`bun lite/oracle.ts\`, against the TypeScript-based harness (\`bun check.ts\`) as the reference. Details in \`tools/lang-experiment/lite/README.md\`.

\`\`\`
existing fixtures       52 of 54 same verdict and reason
                        (reject-mut-capture: lite allows a non-escaping helper, deliberately;
                         reject-no-ts-ignore: lite also reports a type error)
three ports             144 + 25 + 38 cells, all ok   (index reads typed T, as TypeScript does)
                        6 + 3 + 3 rejected            (index reads typed T | undefined)
173 mutants of ports    0 accepted that the oracle rejects
                        2 rejected that the oracle accepts (unary minus on a string)
                        12 unsupported (renamed intrinsic members)
known slips             12 of 13 rejected
check time, CLI         56-63 ms lite, 290-342 ms the TypeScript harness
\`\`\`

In this page:`
// %%
sizes = htl.html`<table class="lite-panel">
<tr><td>bundle loaded by this page (parser, checker, JSX, renderer, per-cell compiler, standard library; no Observable runtime)</td><td>${(ss_bytes.raw / 1024).toFixed(0)} KiB, ${(ss_bytes.gz / 1024).toFixed(0)} KiB gzipped</td></tr>
<tr><td>section 7's 13 cells, check time in this browser, median of 20</td><td>${checkTime.toFixed(1)} ms</td></tr>
</table>`
// %%
md`Limits that apply to everything above:

- No \`keyof\`, indexed access types, tuple rest or variadic inference. A cell that needs one is \`unsupported\`, never \`ok\`.
- A module here holds one \`cells.tsx\` worth of cells. The ports also have \`shell.ts\` (unchecked cells) and \`foreign.d.ts\` (trusted signatures); the checker reads both (\`checkSources\`), this page does not pass them.
- The renderer's event decoders (\`PointerData\` from a \`PointerEvent\`, a string from an \`input\`) are trusted code.
- A \`.tsx\` source has no TypeScript cross-check: the oracle does not read JSX, so the JSX and \`compile\` rules above are checked by \`lite\` alone.`
// %%
md`## Not built: three design directions

These were discussed on 2026-11-14 and none of them exists in \`lite\` or in this page.

**Label, do not reject.** \`Math.random\` above is an error. The alternative is to accept it and label the cell *unsound*, and every cell that takes a capability from it unsound too, by transitivity. A cell that only takes *data* from an unsound cell would stay sound, because a value cannot reach back into the clock. A \`sound(...)\` marker would turn the label back into an error where it matters. Unsound cells would interleave with sound ones in one module rather than living in a separate \`shell.ts\`.

**Islands without the seal.** \`@tomlarkworthy/dataflow-islands\` (\`lopebooks/notebooks/@tomlarkworthy_dataflow-islands.html\`) runs groups of compiled Observable cells in a worker pool behind a runtime seal that, in its own words, "stops accidents, not an author trying to escape". Section 7 does the same for SoundScript cells with the checker's verdict in place of the seal. What is not built is the step between: labelling ordinary lopecode cells sound or unsound, so an island could be cut from a notebook that was not written in SoundScript.

**Reflection.** The typed \`Variable\` and \`Module<C>\` of section 6, so a cell that defines or redefines other cells is checked, and a redefinition rechecks the cells downstream of it. Recorded in \`tools/lang-experiment/lite/NOTES-reflection.md\`; parked.`
// %%
md`## Tests

They run only when this is on, so reading the page costs nothing for them. Each test takes \`tests_on\`, pending until then. The Breakout tests play a copy of section 9's cells (\`ss_instance\`), not the game above.`
// %%
viewof run_tests = Inputs.toggle({ label: "run the tests" })
// %%
tests_on = run_tests ? true : invalidation
// %%
test_examples_check_as_expected = {
  tests_on;
  const want = { cells: [7, 0], errors: [8, 5], mut: [4, 2], counter: [4, 0], reducer: [10, 0], model: [10, 0], workers: [13, 0], rejectJsx: [17, 11], game: [21, 0] };
  const got = {};
  for (const [k, [n, rejected]] of Object.entries(want)) {
    const r = lite.checkSources({ cells: examples[k], tsx: k !== "cells" && k !== "errors" && k !== "mut" });
    const bad = r.cells.filter((c) => c.status === "rejected").length;
    got[k] = [r.cells.length, bad];
    if (r.cells.length !== n || bad !== rejected) throw new Error(`${k}: ${r.cells.length} cells, ${bad} rejected; want ${n}, ${rejected}`);
  }
  return got;
}
// %%
test_compile_pool_matches_main = {
  tests_on;
  const image = await workers_image;
  const main = await workers_onMain({ y0: 0, y1: workers_height });
  if (main.rows.length !== image.length || !main.rows.every((v, i) => v === image[i])) throw new Error("pool and main thread differ");
  return `${image.length} bytes from ${workers_bands.length} pool calls equal one main-thread call`;
}
// %%
test_compile_refuses_unchecked = {
  tests_on;
  // the workers example with one island cell made to read Math.random, which the checker rejects
  const source = examples.workers.replace("out[at + 3] = 255;", "out[at + 3] = Math.random();");
  if (source === examples.workers) throw new Error("rows no longer writes out[at + 3] = 255; update this test");
  const c = ss.compiler(() => source);
  try {
    c.for("inPool", { width: 8, height: 8, maxIter: 10 })({ inputs: ["y0", "y1"], refs: ["width", "height", "maxIter"], outputs: ["rows"] });
  } catch (e) {
    if (!/^compile refused: (\w+, )*rows not ok/.test(e.message)) throw e;
    return e.message;
  }
  throw new Error("compile ran an island with a rejected cell");
}
// %%
test_breakout_subscriptions = {
  tests_on;
  // its own copy of section 9's cells: the game on the page is not played or reset
  const game = await ss_instance(breakout_module, invalidation);
  const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
  const get = (n) => game._scope.get(n)._value;
  for (const n of ["reset", "toggle", "world", "bricks"]) await game.value(n);
  const w0 = get("world"), r0 = ss.running();
  get("toggle")();
  await wait(600);
  const playing = ss.running() - r0, w1 = get("world");
  get("toggle")();
  await wait(200);
  const w2 = get("world"), paused = ss.running() - r0;
  await wait(300);
  const w3 = get("world");
  if (playing !== 1 || w1.ball.y === w0.ball.y) throw new Error(`playing: ${playing} subscriptions, ball ${w0.ball.y} -> ${w1.ball.y}; a hidden tab gets no animation frames`);
  if (paused !== 0 || w3 !== w2) throw new Error(`paused: ${paused} subscriptions, the world ${w3 === w2 ? "kept" : "changed"}`);
  return `play: 1 subscription, the ball moved ${w0.ball.y} -> ${w1.ball.y.toFixed(1)}; pause: none, and the world stays the same object`;
}
// %%
test_breakout_memo = {
  test_breakout_subscriptions; // both count running subscriptions, so they run one after the other
  const game = await ss_instance(breakout_module, invalidation);
  const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
  const get = (n) => game._scope.get(n)._value;
  for (const n of ["reset", "toggle", "world", "bricks", "app"]) await game.value(n);
  const runs = (n) => ss.evals(game._scope.get(n)._definition);
  // the copy's app drawn into a node of its own, as breakout_view draws the page's
  let view = null;
  const drawn = new Promise((res) => invalidation.then(observe(game._scope.get("app"), { fulfilled(a) { view = ss.mount(a, view); res(); } })));
  await drawn;
  const muts = {};
  const mo = new MutationObserver((rs) => { for (const r of rs) { const k = r.type === "attributes" ? "attr " + r.attributeName : r.type; muts[k] = (muts[k] ?? 0) + (r.type === "childList" ? r.addedNodes.length + r.removedNodes.length : 1); } });
  const names = ["ballView", "bricksView", "paddleView", "status", "app", "subscriptions"];
  mo.observe(view, { subtree: true, childList: true, attributes: true, characterData: true });
  const n0 = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n) => [n, runs(n)]));
  const b0 = get("bricks").length;
  let frames = 0, changes = 0, last = get("world");
  get("toggle")();
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await new Promise((res) => { const f = () => { frames++; const w = get("world"); if (w !== last) { changes++; last = w; } if (performance.now() - t0 < 1500) requestAnimationFrame(f); else res(); }; requestAnimationFrame(f); });
  get("toggle")();
  await wait(100);
  mo.disconnect();
  const ran = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n) => [n, runs(n) - n0[n]]));
  const broken = b0 - get("bricks").length;
  if (frames < 20) throw new Error(`only ${frames} frames in 1.5 s; a hidden tab gets no animation frames`);
  if (changes < frames * 0.9) throw new Error(`the world changed ${changes} times in ${frames} frames`);
  if (broken < 1) throw new Error("no brick broke, so the keyed path was not exercised");
  if ((muts["attr style"] ?? 0) > ran.ballView + ran.paddleView + 2) throw new Error(`${muts["attr style"]} style writes for ${ran.ballView + ran.paddleView} ball and paddle draws`);
  if (ran.bricksView > broken) throw new Error(`bricksView ran ${ran.bricksView} times for ${broken} broken bricks`);
  if (ran.paddleView > 0) throw new Error(`paddleView ran ${ran.paddleView} times with no pointer move`);
  if (ran.subscriptions > 2) throw new Error(`subscriptions ran ${ran.subscriptions} times for one play and one pause`);
  if ((muts.childList ?? 0) > broken) throw new Error(`${muts.childList} nodes added or removed for ${broken} broken bricks`);
  return { frames, worldChanges: changes, broken, runs: ran, domMutations: muts };
}
// %%
md`## Appendix: the declarations JSX is checked against

From \`lite/intrinsics.ts\`. A tag not listed is an error (\`<blink>\` in section 8).`
// %%
htl.html`<pre class="lite-src">${lite.JSX_DECLS.trim()}</pre>`
// %%
md`## Appendix: this notebook's code

\`ss\` is imported from \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript\`, which loads it from its attachment \`ss.js.gz\` (built by \`bun lite/soundscript/build.ts\` from \`lite/soundscript/entry.ts\`): the checker, the per-cell compiler, the JSX lowering and renderer, and the standard library that live cells call. \`lite\` is the same object under the name the boxes use. The example sources are attachments of this module.`
// %%
lite = ss
// %%
//raw: ss_bytes
// %%
checkTime = {
  const ts = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
    const t0 = performance.now();
    lite.checkSources({ cells: examples.workers, tsx: true });
    ts.push(performance.now() - t0);
  }
  return ts.sort((a, b) => a - b)[10];
}
// %%
examples = ({
  cells: await FileAttachment("01-cells.ts").text(),
  errors: await FileAttachment("02-errors.ts").text(),
  mut: await FileAttachment("03-mut.ts").text(),
  counter: await FileAttachment("04-counter.tsx").text(),
  reducer: await FileAttachment("05-reducer.tsx").text(),
  model: await FileAttachment("06-model.tsx").text(),
  workers: await FileAttachment("07-workers.tsx").text(),
  rejectJsx: await FileAttachment("08-reject-jsx.tsx").text(),
  game: await FileAttachment("09-breakout.tsx").text()
})
// %%
sourceBox = (value, { rows } = {}) => {
  const box = Inputs.textarea({ value, rows: rows ?? value.split("\n").length + 1, monospace: true, spellcheck: false, width: "100%", resize: true });
  box.classList.add("lite-box");
  return box;
}
// %%
fmt = (v) => {
  if (typeof v === "function") return "ƒ";
  if (typeof v === "number") return Number.isNaN(v) ? "NaN" : String(v);
  if (v === undefined) return "undefined";
  if (ArrayBuffer.isView(v)) return `${v.constructor.name}(${v.length}) [${Array.from(v.slice(0, 8)).join(", ")}${v.length > 8 ? ", …" : ""}]`;
  if (v && typeof v === "object" && "tag" in v && "children" in v) return `<${v.tag || "fragment"}> element`;
  let s;
  try { s = JSON.stringify(v); } catch (e) { s = String(v); }
  return s === undefined ? String(v) : s.length > 90 ? s.slice(0, 87) + "..." : s;
}
// %%
checkBox = (src, { tsx = false } = {}) => {
  let checked;
  try { checked = lite.checkSources({ cells: src, tsx }); }
  catch (e) { return htl.html`<div class="lite-panel lite-err">${e.message}</div>`; }
  const lines = src.split("\n");
  const body = htl.html`<tbody>`;
  for (const c of checked.cells) {
    body.append(htl.html`<tr class=${"lite-" + c.status}><td><code>${c.name}</code></td><td>${c.status}</td><td><code>${c.value ?? ""}</code></td></tr>`);
    const problems = [...c.errors, ...c.unsupported.map((u) => ({ ...u, rule: "unsupported" }))];
    if (c.status === "blocked") problems.push({ line: c.line, rule: "blocked", msg: `by ${c.blockedBy.join(", ")}` });
    for (const e of problems)
      body.append(htl.html`<tr class="lite-err"><td></td><td colspan="2">line ${e.line} <b>[${e.rule}]</b> ${e.msg}<br><code>${(lines[e.line - 1] ?? "").trim()}</code></td></tr>`);
  }
  const count = (s) => checked.cells.filter((c) => c.status === s).length;
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-lesson"><div class="lite-summary">${checked.cells.length} cells: ${count("ok")} ok, ${count("rejected")} rejected, ${count("unsupported")} unsupported, ${count("blocked")} blocked. Checked in ${checked.ms.toFixed(1)} ms.</div>${checked.module.map((e) => htl.html`<div class="lite-err">line ${e.line} <b>[${e.rule}]</b> ${e.msg}</div>`)}
<table class="lite-panel"><thead><tr><th>cell</th><th>check</th><th>type</th></tr></thead>${body}</table></div>`;
}
// %%
kindOf = (v) => {
  const d = String(v._definition);
  if (v._name.startsWith("viewof ")) return "ref cell: the handle";
  if (d.includes("/*ss@ref*/")) return "ref cell: the value";
  if (d.includes('kind: "source"')) return "source: a handle, stepped only by subscriptions";
  if (!d.includes("$ss$.cell(")) return "type";
  const kind = d.includes("memo: true") ? "memoised on its inputs" : "not memoised";
  const extra = /\$ss\$r0/.test(d) ? "; compile refs as inputs" : d.includes("$ss$inv") ? "; holds subscriptions" : "";
  return `cell, ${kind}${extra}`;
}
// %%
liveRows = (module, invalidation, { head, cells, row, cls = "" }) => {
  // A table of module's SoundScript variables, drawn on change only: all rows when the module's code
  // changes (onCodeChange), one row's live part when its variable computes (observe).
  const body = htl.html`<tbody>`;
  let cancels = [], rebuildQueued = false;
  const rebuild = () => {
    rebuildQueued = false;
    cancels.forEach((c) => c());
    const made = cells().map((r) => ({ ...r, ...row(r) }));
    body.replaceChildren(...made.map((r) => r.tr));
    cancels = made.map((r) => observe(r.variable, { fulfilled: () => r.update(), rejected: () => r.update() }));
  };
  const queue = () => { if (!rebuildQueued) { rebuildQueued = true; queueMicrotask(rebuild); } };
  const off = onCodeChange(({ variable, previous }) => { if ((variable ?? previous)?._module === module) queue(); });
  rebuild();
  invalidation.then(() => { off(); cancels.forEach((c) => c()); });
  return htl.html`<table class=${"lite-panel " + cls}>${head}${body}</table>`;
}
// %%
moduleTable = (module, invalidation) => {
  // verdicts come from the module's source, checked again when its code changes
  let source = null, result = null;
  const verdicts = () => { const s = ss_source(module); if (s !== source) { source = s; result = ss.check(s); } return result; };
  return liveRows(module, invalidation, {
    head: htl.html`<thead><tr><th>cell</th><th>check</th><th>type</th><th>value</th><th>runs</th></tr></thead>`,
    cells: () => { const res = verdicts(); return ss_moduleCells(module).filter((c) => !c.key.startsWith("type ")).map((c) => ({ ...c, variable: module._scope.get(c.key) ?? c.variable, verdict: res.cells.find((x) => x.name === c.key) })); },
    row: ({ key, variable: v, verdict: r }) => {
      const val = htl.html`<td class="v">`, runs = htl.html`<td>`;
      const update = () => {
        val.textContent = v._error !== undefined ? `throws: ${v._error?.message ?? v._error}` : fmt(v._value);
        const h = module._scope.get(ss.handleName(key)) ?? v;
        runs.textContent = String(ss.evals(String(h._definition).includes("$ss$.cell(") ? h._definition : v._definition));
      };
      update();
      return { update, tr: htl.html`<tr class=${"lite-" + (r?.status ?? "blocked")}><td><code>${key}</code></td><td>${r?.status ?? ""}</td><td><code>${r?.value ?? ""}</code></td>${val}${runs}</tr>` };
    },
  });
}
// %%
variableTable = (module, invalidation) => liveRows(module, invalidation, {
  cls: "lite-vars",
  head: htl.html`<thead><tr><th>variable</th><th>inputs</th><th>kind</th><th>runs</th></tr></thead>`,
  cells: () => [...runtime._variables].filter((v) => v._module === module && typeof v._name === "string" && typeof v._definition === "function" && ss.langOf(String(v._definition)) === "ss").map((variable) => ({ variable })),
  row: ({ variable: v }) => {
    const counted = String(v._definition).includes("$ss$.cell(");
    const runs = htl.html`<td>`;
    const update = () => { runs.textContent = counted ? String(ss.evals(v._definition)) : ""; };
    update();
    return { update, tr: htl.html`<tr><td><code>${v._name}</code></td><td><code>${v._inputs.map((i) => i._name).join(", ")}</code></td><td>${kindOf(v)}</td>${runs}</tr>` };
  },
})
// %%
benchmark = {
  remeasure;
  const bands = workers_bands, inPool = workers_inPool, onMain = workers_onMain;
  await workers_image;
  const run = (f) => Promise.all(bands.map((b) => f(b)));
  const time = async (f) => { const t0 = performance.now(); await run(f); return performance.now() - t0; };
  const a = await run(inPool), b = await run(onMain);
  const same = a.every((r, i) => r.rows.length === b[i].rows.length && r.rows.every((v, j) => v === b[i].rows[j]));
  for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) { await run(inPool); await run(onMain); }
  const runs = 7, main = [], pool = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < runs; i++) { main.push(await time(onMain)); pool.push(await time(inPool)); }
  const median = (xs) => [...xs].sort((x, y) => x - y)[Math.floor(xs.length / 2)];
  return { poolSize: workers_poolSize, hardware: navigator.hardwareConcurrency, bands: bands.length, runs, mainMs: median(main), poolMs: median(pool), same };
}
